repl_fork — implementation plan
================================

status: IMPLEMENTED (2026-07-23)

references I1..I5 (plans/repl_list). pure pass-through: the dispatcher
entry exists ("fork" => keys ["repl", "new_repl", "state_idx"]), the
handler is one backend.ir call. the interesting part is the tool-
specific semantics around state indices and the sub-repl lifecycle
(shared with repl_merge / repl_truncate / repl_remove).

advertised metadata (the builtin table row)
-------------------------------------------

name          repl_fork
description   "Fork a sub-REPL from an existing REPL at the given
              state index (0 = base state, N = after step N-1,
              -1 = latest). The fork starts with no steps of its own
              and inherits the parent's timeout. Use it to try a
              proof approach without disturbing the parent; bring the
              result back with repl_merge, or discard it with
              repl_remove. Truncating or removing the parent past the
              fork point removes the fork."
              (source: mcp_server.py fork :314, enriched with the
              orphan behavior of ir.ML truncate/remove.)
inputSchema   {"type": "object",
               "properties": {
                 "repl": {"type": "string"},
                 "new_repl": {"type": "string"},
                 "state_idx": {"type": "integer"}},
               "required": ["repl", "new_repl", "state_idx"]}
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": false, "idempotentHint": false,
               "openWorldHint": false}
outputSchema  none.

name/argument mapping across the layers
---------------------------------------

  mcp client edge   tools/call "repl_fork",
                    {"repl": "T", "new_repl": "T2", "state_idx": -1}
  scala builtin row backend.ir("fork", [(repl,T), (new_repl,T2),
                    (state_idx,"-1")]) — integer serialized as its
                    decimal string (json number -> string at the I3
                    conversion; Value.parse_int accepts "-1").
  ml dispatcher     "fork" -> Ir.fork (get "repl") (get "new_repl")
                    (get_int "state_idx")
  engine            ir.ML:456 — negative idx counts from the end
                    (n+1+idx), origin From_REPL (parent, i);
                    out "Forked REPL <new> from <parent> at state <i>"

